00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:06,800
Okay, so let's jump into it.

13
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:13,840
Iframes, of course, are the ability in HTML, it's an iframe tag, or a HTML tag that allows

14
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,800
you to embed another page inside of your website.

15
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:18,800
Okay, so why is that helpful?

16
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,800
Well, there's a lot of reasons why that's helpful.

17
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:25,800
The first one that comes to mind is, of course, clickjacking.

18
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:30,440
Now, you know, when I say clickjacking, I'm sure a lot of you guys are triggered and like,

19
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:31,800
oh, this guy's a scrub.

20
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:32,800
He's talking about clickjacking.

21
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:38,800
Guys, I just want to like, take a second to say, clickjacking can be an incredibly impactful

22
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:39,800
vulnerability, right?

23
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:43,800
You think about one click XSS, we report that all the time.

24
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:47,800
And, you know, this clickjacking can have very, very similar impact.

25
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:53,800
Consider this scenario, you are able to embed an authorization, an OAuth auth flow into your

26
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:54,800
own website, right?

27
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:59,800
With one click on your website, you know, in this invisible iframe, you are able to get

28
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,800
the victim to approve full access to their account, that's full account takeover and one

29
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:03,800
to click, right?

30
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,800
So, clickjacking gets a bad rap.

31
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:10,800
I have submitted plenty of impactful clickjacking reports that have gotten bounties.

32
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:16,800
The reason it gets a bad rap is people will report it without pocking it at all, right?

