68
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:19,800
So, let's go into some of the attributes of iframes so we can understand how they can be manipulated, how they can be used.

69
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:24,800
Okay, the first one is source and source doc.

70
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,800
Obviously, these are going to be the most impactful ones.

71
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:31,800
These are the ones that control where the iframe is pointed at.

72
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:32,800
Okay.

73
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:38,800
So, source simply allows you to point to the source of the iframe at a URL.

74
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:39,800
It could be a JavaScript URL.

75
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:43,800
That's one of the common ways to get XSS is an iframe source JavaScript URL.

76
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:50,800
Could be a, you know, absolute URL where you're pointing to a different website and embedding a victim page.

77
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:54,800
Rarely do we see something like where it's a relative URL.

78
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:57,800
That's just like not super helpful for attackers typically.

79
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,800
And then what is source doc and why do I lump them together?

80
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,800
Well, source doc is sort of the alternative to source.

81
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:11,800
And it is a attribute that allows you to define what should be inside of the iframe directly in the source doc attribute.

82
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,800
And this is extremely helpful for several things.

83
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:23,800
But the first one that pops to mind when I think about the source doc attribute is the fact that you can write a new DOM inside of that iframe.

84
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:30,800
And that is really, really helpful when you need to use a script tag in a post-DOM rendering environment.

85
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:42,800
Because a lot of you guys know that if you try to do an inner HTML sync in DOM XSS, and you try to put a script tag in there, it will not fire because the DOM has already rendered.

86
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:44,800
And that's when script tags trigger.

87
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:49,800
So you can have an inner HTML sync, put a script tag in there, it's not going to do anything.

88
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:59,800
However, and typically you can get around that by using something like a on event handler, like an SVG on load or an image on error handler.

89
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:09,800
However, sometimes you are limited, or maybe you need to import a different origin, right, something like that, or import your script from a different origin.

90
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:11,800
And you want to use a script tag.

91
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:15,800
Well, you're kind of stuck until iframe source doc comes in.

92
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:20,800
So what you can actually do is iframe source doc, and then inside of that source doc, you can define the script tag.

93
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:27,800
And since that iframe is in a new DOM inside of the iframe, that script tag will trigger, resulting in XSS.

94
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,800
Okay, so it's a little bit of a roundabout way, but you can still accomplish the same goal.

95
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:40,800
And it has saved my ass a couple times because sometimes there's just a really weird filter on there that doesn't allow for, you know, any sort of on event handlers,

96
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,800
or maybe there's a character limitation or something like that.

97
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,800
This one does work.

98
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,800
So very helpful.

99
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:55,800
The other thing is that source doc will be same origin as the parent when it's defined.

100
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:04,800
So that is helpful for just being able to embed content inside the page in an isolated DOM that is still the same origin as the parent.

101
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,800
