00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:58,540
Now, the first one is called window.name hijacking.

427
00:21:58,800 --> 00:21:58,960
Okay.

428
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:01,800
And this one is a little bit tricky to understand.

429
00:22:04,340 --> 00:22:06,380
But I'm going to do my best over the audio medium.

430
00:22:06,740 --> 00:22:06,960
Okay.

431
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:07,800
Consider this.

432
00:22:07,940 --> 00:22:11,720
You have an attacker-controlled page and you window.open a victim page.

433
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:20,100
The victim then clicks a button on that page, which triggers another window.open with a very specific name that you know the value of.

434
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:29,900
What attacker can then do is create an iframe on their own page before the button is clicked, just to be clear, that has that same name.

435
00:22:29,900 --> 00:22:41,440
Now, the victim page and the attacker page are in a linked browsing context because of the opener relationship between those two pages.

436
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:47,820
And because they share the same browsing context, they will also be able to reference each other's frames.

437
00:22:47,820 --> 00:23:08,180
And what will happen then is if an attacker knows the origin that the victim will be opening the tab into, so if they're going to do a window.open on like site.com or whatever, then they can embed their own attacker iframe inside of the attacker-controlled page that points to site.com that is on site.com.

438
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:19,780
Maybe you find a 404 page or something that doesn't have X-frame options on it, and you embed it in your page, and you name that iframe the same thing as the victim will be opening with window.open.

439
00:23:20,360 --> 00:23:20,600
Okay.

440
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:26,840
And what's going to happen in that scenario is when the user clicks the button and it opens the pop-up, it is not going to pop up at all.

441
00:23:27,080 --> 00:23:34,900
It is going to open that attacker or that victim's new pop-up window into your iframe that's on your attacker-controlled page.

442
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:47,160
Now, the reason why that is interesting is that there's often a lot of implicit relationship stuff that occurs between opened windows and their opener.

443
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:56,960
So the opener will take that frame reference and be like, all right, I'm just going to use this frame reference as the source of truth as whether I should trust this post message or not.

444
00:23:57,240 --> 00:24:04,800
Or when, you know, I'm going to keep checking the origin of that window reference, and when it comes back to my origin, then I'm going to trust the data that comes out of it.

445
00:24:04,800 --> 00:24:07,140
You know, I'm going to grab from like the hash or something like that.

446
00:24:07,320 --> 00:24:08,260
I've seen that a couple of times.

447
00:24:09,220 --> 00:24:13,620
So what you can do by hijacking that frame, obviously, you're the parent of that frame.

448
00:24:13,620 --> 00:24:14,600
It's in your own page.

449
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:18,040
So you control, you know, where that frame is pointed to.

450
00:24:18,240 --> 00:24:28,640
So you simply redirect that frame to an attacker-controlled page and send the post message back to its opener saying, hey, yo, I'm the frame you opened.

451
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:33,680
Here's the data, you know, and pass it through and exploit it that way.

452
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:46,540
Or if it's looking to, you know, get a callback or something like that on that frame, then, you know, you get whatever data you want to call back, throw it in the hash, and then redirect it back to whatever callback page the opener was expecting.

453
00:24:46,540 --> 00:24:50,700
And it will parse it just like, you know, it just coming back from the call that it did.

454
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:53,560
So this is a very cool attack.

455
00:24:53,660 --> 00:24:55,280
I've exploited this several times.

456
00:24:55,280 --> 00:24:57,060
I think probably less than 10 times.

457
00:24:57,060 --> 00:24:59,380
But still, very, very fun attack.

458
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:01,920
You know, when it works, it's a blast.

459
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:07,320
And I just love that we can control where a pop-up goes, right?

460
00:25:07,400 --> 00:25:08,500
Like, isn't that such a cool thing?

461
00:25:08,560 --> 00:25:13,660
The victim is expecting sort of a pop-up to happen, and instead it just opens into an attacker-controlled iframe.

462
00:25:13,900 --> 00:25:20,820
And now you're focused on the attacker's page, and your iframe now is populated with that victim's data that they were trying to open up.

463
00:25:21,340 --> 00:25:21,940
Very cool.

