00:26:10,820 --> 00:26:17,040
This episode goes to production pretty quickly, so I'm not sure we're going to have time to this, but I'll try to explain it anyway.

478
00:26:17,040 --> 00:26:20,520
Okay, so we've got an attacker frame, right?

479
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:21,740
And a victim frame.

480
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:35,040
And what happens in this scenario is that the attacker gets an XSS on an origin that is iframed in a victim tab.

481
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:35,540
Okay?

482
00:26:35,720 --> 00:26:39,120
So consider the scenario where you've got...

483
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:49,680
One of the real examples from my experiences was I was able to pop an XSS on the origin that controls the user's mail settings, right?

484
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:52,240
Like, you know, do you want to subscribe to our newsletter or whatever, right?

485
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:57,840
It's like sometimes those things are super easy to pop and they're embedded in an iframe on like www, right?

486
00:26:59,820 --> 00:27:01,820
So you're like, okay, what can I do with this?

487
00:27:01,860 --> 00:27:02,800
It isn't really in scope.

488
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:04,140
I want to affect www.

489
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:05,260
What do I do?

490
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:07,480
Well, here's what you do.

491
00:27:07,780 --> 00:27:08,740
So you...

492
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:11,420
From the attacker's page, you open a frame.

493
00:27:11,560 --> 00:27:12,360
That's the victim page.

494
00:27:12,440 --> 00:27:14,240
The victim page has that...

495
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:17,680
I'm going to call it the mail domain, iframed into it.

496
00:27:17,840 --> 00:27:25,660
Now, as the attacker, you iframe on your page, your XSS into the mail to the mail domain, right?

497
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:31,520
So now both pages have an iframe to the mail domain and they're open and they have an opener relationship with one another, right?

498
00:27:31,820 --> 00:27:33,980
I guess one of them has an opener relationship.

499
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:35,540
One of them has an open E relationship.

500
00:27:36,380 --> 00:27:44,100
So what you then do is from the attacker's frame, inside of the iframe, you go up to the attacker's top level page.

501
00:27:44,860 --> 00:27:48,500
Over to the victim tab and down into the victim's iframe.

502
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:57,500
Now, these two iframed pages have the same origin, which means they can access each other's DOM.

503
00:27:57,860 --> 00:28:05,620
So the attacker with the malicious XSS then affects the DOM within that page or grabs whatever information is out of it on that page.

504
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:14,540
In my scenario, there was a bunch of PII leaked into that from the parent when it tells the mail domain, hey, this is the user I want you to control the mail for.

505
00:28:15,320 --> 00:28:17,100
And you get impact that way.

506
00:28:17,240 --> 00:28:17,860
Let me grab some water.

