533
00:30:00,780 --> 00:30:06,700
Last but not least, a couple of fun facts about iframes that have just landed me a couple bugs recently.

534
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:12,220
This is a fun one from the critical research lab, actually.

535
00:30:12,780 --> 00:30:19,840
The content security policies frame ancestor attribute will override Xframe options header.

536
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:20,880
Okay.

537
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:21,740
So let me say that again.

538
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:25,100
The Xframe options header, of course, you guys know what the Xframe options header is.

539
00:30:25,800 --> 00:30:29,940
It has, you know, it controls whether a page can be iframed or not.

540
00:30:29,940 --> 00:30:33,540
So what you'll often see is Xframe options deny, right?

541
00:30:33,700 --> 00:30:35,780
So this page cannot be framed at all.

542
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:36,920
GG, game over.

543
00:30:36,920 --> 00:30:52,900
Well, if there's a content security policy that has a frame ancestor attribute in it, and that defines, you know, some pages that the page can be iframed from, then that will override Xframe options and be given priority.

544
00:30:52,900 --> 00:31:01,220
Which is very interesting because a lot of times what happens in browser security is if there's a conflict between two, you know, security related entities.

545
00:31:01,220 --> 00:31:07,380
And the one that they go for is the one that is more safe, the one that is least permissive, right?

546
00:31:07,540 --> 00:31:13,160
But in this scenario, we actually have it going with the one that is, that is, I'm sorry, yeah, the one that's least permissive.

547
00:31:13,260 --> 00:31:16,000
In this scenario, we have one going for the one that is more permissive.

548
00:31:16,680 --> 00:31:17,320
Kind of odd.

549
00:31:17,320 --> 00:31:18,320
Okay.

550
00:31:18,320 --> 00:31:18,360
Okay.

